Tim Burton brings his vision of a dark  dreamland to the big screen and a  live-action remake of Dumbo but what did  he change from the original movie and  how does it compare to the animated  classic epic I may be lovers I'm Jan and  today I'm explaining the top 12 biggest  differences between Disney's 1941 Dumbo  movie and the brand new live-action  Dumbo film plus I'll be revealing some  Easter eggs to the original movie you  may have missed one of the biggest  changes in the live-action movie is that  there are no talking animals that means  that Dumbo's best friend Timothy Q Mouse  is no longer part of the story though we  do get a nod to him as two of the  movie's new characters Millie and Joe  have a little mouse circus and not only  as one of their mice dressed like the  animated rodents but we even see it eat  a peanut just like the first time we met  Timothy in the classic film Millie and  Joe in fact take on the role that  Timothy played as Dumbo's confidant and  champion so they stand up for Dumbo when  he's mistreated comfort him when his  mother's locked up and help him with his  flying in keeping with the filmmakers  desire to make the world feel as real as  possible  Dumbo's mother mrs. jumbo is actually  pregnant with her baby in the  live-action remake meaning there are no  baby delivering storks though there is  an Easter Egg to their animated role as  we see them in the new movie just before  we glimpse baby Dumbo for the very first  time like in the original mrs. jumbo  protects her young son from bullying and  threats which once again lands her in  elephant jail though on this occasion  the filmmakers up the stakes  making the consequences for shielding  her son even more dire though they also  add a modern twist to her venture  reunion with her little one there's no  chorus of female elephants to criticize  and ridicule Dumbo this time but he does  get more than enough of that from some  of the circus workers crowd and even  Danny DeVito's ringmaster at first  talking stalks elephants and mice aren't  the only creatures cut from the  live-action film also can do the crows  who helped young Dumbo fly and their use  of feathers and psychology to encourage  his aerial abilities is taken over by  Joe and Millie the crows absence from  the new movie is hardly surprising given  they've been heavily criticized as  racist stereotypes of African Americans  there is a little Easter Egg to their  fame  seong when I see an elephant fly though  when the dreamland ringmaster introduces  Dumbo's act with a slight variation on  the following lines from the crows most  of the live-action films new characters  a human but there is one notable new  animal addition a monkey who just like  the other animals never talks and is  completely computer-generated the new  CGI primate reminds me of the popular  real-life one that featured in the night  of the museum movies and like that  capuchin monkey this new mischievous  monkeys role is to add some comic relief  which it does plenty of by performing  both Shakespeare and damsel in distress  routines as part of the circus and also  by popping up in some surprising places  so the monkey near death just for  emergencies just like the star of the  animated movie the computer-generated  lead of the live-action film was  entirely adorable and despite the many  tweaks to the airborne elephant story  such as the origin of his name and how  we find out he can fly Dumbo's character  remains as heartwarming and moving as  before which means his adventures will  likely break your heart before finally  putting it back together again and the  film's core messages of self-belief and  the differences strength not weakness  are still very much there indeed they're  further highlighted by Dumbo's adopted  human family the classic movie last for  mere 64 minutes compared to the new  films almost two hour runtime so it's  hardly surprising this plenty of new  material and Tim Burton's reimagining of  the tale in fact the scene where Dumbo  flies down from a burning building at  the end of the original film is  recreated about a third of the way into  the live-action movie and is really the  jumping-off point for a whole new  strands the flying elephant story which  is now set just after the first world  war rather than during the Second World  War when the animated film was made in  another change this time Dumbo doesn't  get drunk on champagne and hallucinate  pink elephants however there is a  gorgeous version of that memorable  moment as a circus performers make  bubble elephants that multiply and dance  there's even a suggestion that the new  baby Dumbo gets mesmerized watching the  performance as he nods along to the  music with an entranced look on his face  when it comes to songs Dumbo takes a  leaf out of Disney's live-action  Cinderella rather than their recent take  on Beauty and the Beast with only one of  the full songs from the animation making  it into the new film actress Sharon  Mooney who plays a circus mermaid gives  a sweet performance of baby mind the  beautiful lullaby that accompanies an  emotional scene between Dumbo and his  mother and you should listen out for  some nice Easter eggs to the animations  other famous songs including a nod to  pink elephants on parade as well as  Danny DeVito singing a couple of lines  from Casey jr. Plus during the credits  you'll hear different versions of those  songs again as well as a jazzy  instrumental of when I see an elephant  fly with the lack of talking animals the  human characters in Burton's movie  become much more important Danny DeVito  is great as a struggling circus owner  max Medici who has a bigger role in the  new film than the original ringmaster  initially Medici wants to hide Dumbo's  large ears from the circus going public  and although he echos some of the  animated elephants mean words and locks  up to mrs. jumbo he turns out to be a  much more obviously sympathetic  character than his animated counterpart  that he tries to do the best he can for  his large circus family of strongmen  mermaid snake charmers and more the  addition of Michael Keaton's ba  VanDerveer is a major change in the  original animation where although the  chorus of elephants are incredibly nasty  too Dumbo and the tormenting boys are  also horribly cruel there's no one  standout villain in contrast Keaton's  ruthless entrepreneur whose desperate to  buy Dumbo circus so he can exploit the  miraculous pachyderm and make a mountain  of money adds a new and interesting plot  to this version it also takes us to  dreamland a theme park that VanDerveer  sees as a future of entertainment but  which has a much darker side something  else the filmmakers wanted to achieve  with the human characters was that Dumbo  story would be a way for them to reflect  on their own situations so for example  former circus star and wounded War  veteran halt farriers relationship with  his children parallels the relationship  between Dumbo and mrs. jumbo because  just as young Dumbo is trying to  reconnect with his mum so to halt and  his children are trying to put their  family back together after being forced  to part by the  or million Joe also know how it feels to  have lost a mother which adds an extra  dimension to the bond they form with  Dumbo although the human characters are  a neat way for the filmmakers to  differentiate this version of Dumbo from  the original they're an area where the  new movie can fall a little flat even  though they get more screen time than  their animated counterparts like Timothy  Q Mouse generally they don't make the  same impact the issue isn't the cast  list which is suitably impressive  including Colin Farrell Eva Green and  Alan Arkin the thing is that there are  almost too many new human characters to  the point that they're pretty thinly  drawn still this is Dumbo story and  there's still a lot of sweetness tears  and joy to be found in this beautifully  designed world now it's your turn to let  me know which is your favorite Dumbo  movie tapping the top right and after  you've cast your vote you'll be able to  see the results if you enjoy deep dive  theories and behind-the-scenes secrets  about your favorite Disney movies be  sure to subscribe and hit the bell to  get all my new videos thanks for  watching and see you next time  yippee-ki-yay movie lovers
 

